No cold-side design reaches the 259.7 us bar (304.67 - 45).
- Best realistic cold-side stack: about 275-290 us (+0.9-1.8% headline):
  - lane Q v4cxs: -3..-5 us;
  - fused cold engine: -8..-15 us;
  - wgmma-hot threshold 32: 0..-15 us.
- A perfect cold engine (100% of the ring, zero fixed cost) is not reachable and would still give about 264 us at the judge's draw mix.

mechanism:
NO DESIGN ON THIS ANGLE CLEARS THE BAR (headline 0).

1) The "blocked 170 us" is SM contention, not idle time. In the node E3 traces (m03c r1/r2, 40/40 reps), the fork runs as a 4-phase interleave:
- align 0-11 us;
- K1 (ea4_w13) runs alone for a median 123 us;
- wg13 starts in K1's tail, K2 in wg13's tail, wg2 in K2's tail;
- the post-norm runs last.
Each phase's tail is already filled by the other half. The cold half's 170 us is its own SM-time and has to be spent somewhere. That is why M03c found that "every knob trades blocking for path time".

2) Only idle SM-time can be recovered. There are four sources:
- the head: align 10.1 us on 33 CTAs, plus gather 3.1;
- head-of-line half-SM idle in the cold tails, because a wg CTA needs a whole SM;
- the end phase, where wg2 runs alone with static stride and staggered starts (node median 26 us, range 9-46);
- the post-norm, 5.7 us.
The judge's fork already gains only 9.4 / 13.5 / 21.1 us over serial (p50 / p10 / p90), so the hot half's internal idle is modest at T=256. The W5 judge agrees: forcing more hot items (N64) at T=256 was slower, 135.1 vs D0 127.3 us.

3) Model results. I ran a numpy model of the judge routing (Student-t popularity; it reproduces the gate draws: p50 38 passes, 6-7 hot experts, ~270 hot rows). Hot items are calibrated to the measured hot-alone times (110 / 142 / 170 us) and cold to 27.4 + 7.34 us per pass. Over 400 draws, against the measured fork, the only design that approaches perfect packing gives:
- realistic list schedule: x1.06-1.15, i.e. -18 to -41 us, central about -27 us (+1.6% headline);
- zero-overhead ceiling: -32 to -54 us, central about -41 us (+2.44%);
- fluid bound: -37 to -60 us.
That design is one persistent launch: role-pure CTAs time-multiplexed between hot wgmma items and cold Engine A pieces, LPT order, per-chunk w13 to w2 and per-pass K1 to K2 dependencies. The bar is -45 us (x1.173, +2.70%). Only the most favourable calibration reaches it, and only at zero overhead.

4) The other sub-angles are dead:
- Re-hot/cold thresholds: H=16 already minimizes per-expert SM-time at every route count. In us-GPU, Engine A costs 7.3/8.6 against wg 11.7 at 16 or fewer routes, and wg wins above that (12.9 vs 16-17 at 17-32 routes; 16.1 vs 25-34 at 33-64; 20.4 vs 41-69 at 65-128). The judge races agree (H-8f x1.001, H-116f x0.973 vs H-16f x1.073), and the threshold sweep was declared exhausted (LOG:718-719).
- Hot work splits: refuted (wg2, wgx/H-20, exact-N).
- Stream-K across the halves in one CTA: that is K6 mixing, x1.57-1.82 slower.
- Static SM splits: x1.10-1.64 slower (LOG:1073-1074).
